% Forward pipeline operator (.>) desugars at parse time into a function call.
%
% Normative. Every implementation must pass every case.

### name: pipe.ident.bare
--- source
"hello" .> UPPER
--- expect
text "HELLO"
===

### name: pipe.ident.empty-parens
--- source
"hello" .> UPPER()
--- expect
text "HELLO"
===

### name: pipe.chain.two
--- source
"  hello  " .> TRIM .> UPPER
--- expect
text "HELLO"
===

### name: pipe.chain.three
--- source
"  hello  " .> TRIM .> UPPER .> LEN
--- expect
num 5
===

### name: pipe.args.thread-first
--- source
"hello world" .> LEFT(5)
--- expect
text "hello"
===

### name: pipe.args.multiple
--- source
"hello world" .> SUBSTR(1, 5)
--- expect
text "hello"
===

### name: pipe.placeholder.second
--- source
"," .> JOIN((1, 2, 3), _)
--- expect
text "1,2,3"
===

### name: pipe.placeholder.first
--- source
"hello" .> LEFT(_, 4)
--- expect
text "hell"
===

### name: pipe.aggregate.filter
--- source
(1, 2, 3, 4) .> FILTER(_ % 2 == 0) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,4"
===

### name: pipe.aggregate.filter.binder
--- source
(1, 2, 3, 4) .> FILTER(X, X > 2) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "3,4"
===

### name: pipe.aggregate.map
--- source
(1, 2, 3) .> MAP(_ * 2) .> JOIN(",")
--- expect
text "2,4,6"
===

### name: pipe.aggregate.sum
--- source
(10, 20, 30) .> SUM(_)
--- expect
num 60
===

### name: pipe.count
--- source
(1, 2, 3) .> COUNT
--- expect
num 3
===

### name: pipe.precedence.compare
--- source
(1, 2, 3) .> COUNT > 0
--- expect
bool TRUE
===

### name: pipe.precedence.logical-and
--- source
(1, 2) .> COUNT > 0 AND (3, 4) .> COUNT > 0
--- expect
bool TRUE
===

### name: pipe.precedence.add
--- source
(1, 2) .> COUNT + 10
--- expect
num 12
===

### name: pipe.precedence.concat
--- source
"hello" .> UPPER & " world"
--- expect
text "HELLO world"
===

### name: pipe.parenthesised-lhs
--- source
(2 + 3) .> UPPER
--- expect
text "5"
===

### name: pipe.indexed-lhs
--- source
L = ("hello", "world"); L[1] .> UPPER
--- expect
text "HELLO"
===

### name: pipe.indexed-rhs
--- source
"a,b,c" .> SPLIT(",") [2]
--- expect
text "b"
===

### name: pipe.assignment.target
--- source
A = "hi" .> UPPER; A
--- expect
text "HI"
===

### name: pipe.assign-target-error
--- source
5 .> UPPER = 1
--- expect
error E_BAD_ASSIGN at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.syntax.rhs-number
--- source
5 .> 10
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.syntax.rhs-text
--- source
5 .> "hello"
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.syntax.rhs-paren
--- source
5 .> (1 + 2)
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.syntax.rhs-bool
--- source
5 .> TRUE
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.syntax.rhs-null
--- source
5 .> NULL
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.syntax.trailing-eof
--- source
5 .>
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===

### name: pipe.syntax.trailing-semicolon
--- source
5 .> ; 1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.unknown-func
--- source
5 .> UNKNOWN_FUNC
--- expect
error E_UNKNOWN_FUNC at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.arity.too-few
--- source
5 .> LEFT
--- expect
error E_ARITY at 1:6
===

### name: pipe.arity.too-many
--- source
5 .> UPPER(1)
--- expect
error E_ARITY at 1:6
===
### name: pipe.placeholder.repeated
--- note
Every top-level bare `_` is replaced by the piped value, not only the first (SPEC grammar, pipelines). Go once stopped after the first, leaving the second a variable read.
--- source
3 .> MAX(_, _)
--- expect
num 3
===

### name: pipe.placeholder.repeated-three
--- note
Three placeholders, all replaced.
--- source
3 .> MAX(_, _, _)
--- expect
num 3
===

### name: pipe.placeholder.repeated-evaluates-once-per-placeholder
--- note
The replacement is textual, so the piped expression is evaluated once per placeholder, in argument order: the side effect runs twice and N ends at 2.
--- source
N = 0; (N = N + 1) .> MAX(_, _) & "/" & N
--- expect
text "2/2"
===

### name: pipe.placeholder.below-min-arity-is-a-plain-argument
--- note
SUBSTR needs two arguments, so `SUBSTR(_)` has too few for the placeholder rule to apply: the piped value is prepended and the `_` stays a variable read.
--- source
"abc" .> SUBSTR(_)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:17
===

### name: pipe.placeholder.binding-function-is-not-substituted
--- note
A binding built-in never substitutes: its second argument is the binder name, so a `_` there is not a placeholder and the piped value is always prepended.
--- source
(1,2,3) .> FILTER((4,5,6), _)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:20
===
